# Code Standards

## TypeScript
- Use TypeScript for all code — no plain JS files
- Prefer interfaces over types
- Define types for all props, params, and return values
- Use descriptive names with auxiliary verbs: isLoading, hasError, isVisible, shouldShow

## Component Patterns
- Functional components only — no classes
- Use consts: const MyComponent = () => rather than function MyComponent()
- Favor named exports over default exports where possible
- Break components into smaller parts with minimal props
- Use composition to build complex UI from simple components
- File structure order: exported component, subcomponents, helpers, static content, types
- Place static content and interfaces at end of file
- Keep pages split: PageClient.tsx (use client) + page.tsx (metadata + server export)

## Code Quality
- DRY principle — never duplicate logic, modularize repeated patterns
- Use early returns for error conditions and guard clauses
- Avoid unnecessary else — use if-return pattern
- Avoid deeply nested conditionals
- Event functions named with handle prefix: handleClick, handleKeyDown
- Use lowercase with dashes for directories: components/auth-wizard
- File extensions: .config.ts, .test.ts, .context.tsx, .type.ts, .hook.ts

## Next.js
- Minimize use of 'use client' — favor React Server Components
- Use 'use client' only for components that need browser APIs or animations
- Wrap client components in Suspense with fallback
- Use dynamic imports for non-critical heavy components
- Optimize images: WebP format, explicit sizes, lazy loading via next/image
- Prioritize Web Vitals: LCP, CLS, FID

## Rules
- Leave NO todos, placeholders, or missing pieces
- Include all required imports
- Reference file names explicitly
- Do not install new npm packages without asking first
- If something is uncertain, say so instead of guessing